Subject: [PATCH 0005] Pad the QSBR thread state to a multiple of 64, not to 64
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 domain (musl)
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

struct _qsbr_pad (Include/internal/pycore_qsbr.h) pads the per-thread QSBR
state to 64 bytes against false sharing with
char __padding[64 - sizeof(struct _qsbr_thread_state)]. It holds three
pointers, so with 16-byte capabilities the state exceeds 64 bytes, the size
wraps and the array is "too large (18446744073709551584 elements)" -- in every
file that includes pycore_interp.h (159 in the fifth survey).

The padding now rounds up to the next multiple of 64. For every state smaller
than 64 bytes, which is every platform upstream supports, the expression gives
exactly the old size. The padding is a performance measure; its size does not
affect correctness.

--- a/Include/internal/pycore_qsbr.h
+++ b/Include/internal/pycore_qsbr.h
@@ -70,10 +70,12 @@
     struct _qsbr_thread_state *freelist_next;
 };
 
-// Padding to avoid false sharing
+// Padding to avoid false sharing: round up to a multiple of 64 bytes. With
+// 16-byte capability pointers the state itself is larger than 64.
 struct _qsbr_pad {
     struct _qsbr_thread_state qsbr;
-    char __padding[64 - sizeof(struct _qsbr_thread_state)];
+    char __padding[64 * (sizeof(struct _qsbr_thread_state) / 64 + 1)
+                   - sizeof(struct _qsbr_thread_state)];
 };
 
 // Per-interpreter state
